{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ vogue \"more inclusive\" (+ audience readers readership sales circulation popular popularity))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_143174827#2_314969493","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q309","rank":1,"snippet":"industry to adapt By Bella Web b After years of creating custom clothing in a wide range of sizes, “we decided to give consistency to this offer and officially expand the size offering of our collections”, says Stefano Gabbana in an interview with <cover>Vogue Business. “ We never looked at fashion and beauty standards. What we have always been interested in is dressing authentic, beautiful women without conforming to any rules.” Dolce & Gabbana does not release sales figures, but 11 Honoré, an online retailer specialising in plus-size designer clothing, saw a 70 and 80 per cent sell-through rate for Dolce & Gabbana’s first two collections, respectively. There is a long way to go to amend the broader luxury differential between representation on the catwalk shows and plus-size clothing in stores. Other brands are entering the market, though they are mostly independent labels, like Mary Katrantzou and Diane von Furstenberg. Carolina Herrera is trialling more inclusive </cover>sizes, while designers like Adam Lippes and Christian Siriano are sold on 11 Honoré, which launched in 2016 with 15 brands and today carries more than 80 brands in sizes US 12-22. “\" , \"start_char\": 1375.000000"}}
